  "analyzed_vuln": {
    "function": "audio_sample_entry_box_read",
    "file": "gpac/src/isomedia/box_code_base.c",
    "description": "The malformed-audio recovery path incorrectly assumes `ptr->esd` is a direct child of the MP4A sample entry. During normal child handling, `audio_sample_entry_on_child_box` may set `ptr->esd` to an ESDS box that remains owned by a nested QuickTime `wave` box. When child parsing later fails, `audio_sample_entry_box_read` calls `gf_isom_box_del_parent(&ptr->child_boxes, ptr->esd)`. Because the ESDS is not in the MP4A child list, removal does nothing, but `gf_isom_box_del_parent` still frees it. The pointer remains in the wave box's child list; closing the file traverses that list and calls `gf_isom_box_del` on the freed ESDS, causing the reported heap-use-after-free. The root cause is the wrong-owner deletion at line 4289, not the eventual read in `gf_isom_box_del`.",
    "conditions": "A crafted input parsed by `gf_isom_open_file` must create an MP4A audio sample entry containing a QuickTime `wave` child with a successfully instantiated ESDS child, so `audio_sample_entry_on_child_box` aliases that nested ESDS into `ptr->esd` without moving it out of `wave->child_boxes`. The MP4A child-array parse must then return an error (for example, the PoC's truncated/malformed `wave`/`esds` sizing or descriptor payload), causing `audio_sample_entry_box_read` to enter its raw-data ESDS recovery scan. Finding the `esds` signature triggers deletion through the MP4A child list, leaving the nested wave list dangling. A subsequent `gf_isom_close` destroys the MP4A/wave hierarchy and re-deletes the stale ESDS pointer."
  },
